36 {\cf20  *  The ANSI C standard committee, for the C99 standard, specified the}\par
37 {\cf20  *  inclusion of a new standard include file called stdint.h.  This is}\par
38 {\cf20  *  a very useful and long desired include file which contains several}\par
39 {\cf20  *  very precise definitions for integer scalar types that is}\par
40 {\cf20  *  critically important for making portable several classes of}\par
41 {\cf20  *  applications including cryptography, hashing, variable length}\par
42 {\cf20  *  integer libraries and so on.  But for most developers its likely}\par
43 {\cf20  *  useful just for programming sanity.}\par
44 {\cf20  *}\par
45 {\cf20  *  The problem is that some compiler vendors chose to ignore the C99}\par
46 {\cf20  *  standard and some older compilers have no opportunity to be updated.}\par
47 {\cf20  *  Because of this situation, simply including stdint.h in your code}\par
48 {\cf20  *  makes it unportable.}\par
49 {\cf20  *}\par
50 {\cf20  *  So that's what this file is all about.  Its an attempt to build a}\par
51 {\cf20  *  single universal include file that works on as many platforms as}\par
52 {\cf20  *  possible to deliver what stdint.h is supposed to.  Even compilers}\par
53 {\cf20  *  that already come with stdint.h can use this file instead without}\par
54 {\cf20  *  any loss of functionality.  A few things that should be noted about}\par
55 {\cf20  *  this file:}\par
56 {\cf20  *}\par
57 {\cf20  *    1) It is not guaranteed to be portable and/or present an identical}\par
58 {\cf20  *       interface on all platforms.  The extreme variability of the}\par
59 {\cf20  *       ANSI C standard makes this an impossibility right from the}\par
60 {\cf20  *       very get go. Its really only meant to be useful for the vast}\par
61 {\cf20  *       majority of platforms that possess the capability of}\par
62 {\cf20  *       implementing usefully and precisely defined, standard sized}\par
63 {\cf20  *       integer scalars.  Systems which are not intrinsically 2s}\par
64 {\cf20  *       complement may produce invalid constants.}\par
65 {\cf20  *}\par
66 {\cf20  *    2) There is an unavoidable use of non-reserved symbols.}\par
67 {\cf20  *}\par
68 {\cf20  *    3) Other standard include files are invoked.}\par
69 {\cf20  *}\par
70 {\cf20  *    4) This file may come in conflict with future platforms that do}\par
71 {\cf20  *       include stdint.h.  The hope is that one or the other can be}\par
72 {\cf20  *       used with no real difference.}\par
73 {\cf20  *}\par
74 {\cf20  *    5) In the current version, if your platform can't represent}\par
75 {\cf20  *       int32_t, int16_t and int8_t, it just dumps out with a compiler}\par
76 {\cf20  *       error.}\par
77 {\cf20  *}\par
78 {\cf20  *    6) 64 bit integers may or may not be defined.  Test for their}\par
79 {\cf20  *       presence with the test: #ifdef INT64_MAX or #ifdef UINT64_MAX.}\par
80 {\cf20  *       Note that this is different from the C99 specification which}\par
81 {\cf20  *       requires the existence of 64 bit support in the compiler.  If}\par
82 {\cf20  *       this is not defined for your platform, yet it is capable of}\par
83 {\cf20  *       dealing with 64 bits then it is because this file has not yet}\par
84 {\cf20  *       been extended to cover all of your system's capabilities.}\par
85 {\cf20  *}\par
86 {\cf20  *    7) (u)intptr_t may or may not be defined.  Test for its presence}\par
87 {\cf20  *       with the test: #ifdef PTRDIFF_MAX.  If this is not defined}\par
88 {\cf20  *       for your platform, then it is because this file has not yet}\par
89 {\cf20  *       been extended to cover all of your system's capabilities, not}\par
90 {\cf20  *       because its optional.}\par
91 {\cf20  *}\par
92 {\cf20  *    8) The following might not been defined even if your platform is}\par
93 {\cf20  *       capable of defining it:}\par
94 {\cf20  *}\par
95 {\cf20  *       WCHAR_MIN}\par
96 {\cf20  *       WCHAR_MAX}\par
97 {\cf20  *       (u)int64_t}\par
98 {\cf20  *       PTRDIFF_MIN}\par
99 {\cf20  *       PTRDIFF_MAX}\par
100 {\cf20  *       (u)intptr_t}\par
101 {\cf20  *}\par
102 {\cf20  *    9) The following have not been defined:}\par
103 {\cf20  *}\par
104 {\cf20  *       WINT_MIN}\par
105 {\cf20  *       WINT_MAX}\par
106 {\cf20  *}\par
107 {\cf20  *   10) The criteria for defining (u)int_least(*)_t isn't clear,}\par
108 {\cf20  *       except for systems which don't have a type that precisely}\par
109 {\cf20  *       defined 8, 16, or 32 bit types (which this include file does}\par
110 {\cf20  *       not support anyways). Default definitions have been given.}\par
111 {\cf20  *}\par
112 {\cf20  *   11) The criteria for defining (u)int_fast(*)_t isn't something I}\par
113 {\cf20  *       would trust to any particular compiler vendor or the ANSI C}\par
114 {\cf20  *       committee.  It is well known that "compatible systems" are}\par
115 {\cf20  *       commonly created that have very different performance}\par
116 {\cf20  *       characteristics from the systems they are compatible with,}\par
117 {\cf20  *       especially those whose vendors make both the compiler and the}\par
118 {\cf20  *       system.  Default definitions have been given, but its strongly}\par
119 {\cf20  *       recommended that users never use these definitions for any}\par
120 {\cf20  *       reason (they do *NOT* deliver any serious guarantee of}\par
121 {\cf20  *       improved performance -- not in this file, nor any vendor's}\par
122 {\cf20  *       stdint.h).}\par
123 {\cf20  *}\par
124 {\cf20  *   12) The following macros:}\par
125 {\cf20  *}\par
126 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INTMAX_MODIFIER}\par
127 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT64_MODIFIER}\par
128 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT32_MODIFIER}\par
129 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT16_MODIFIER}\par
130 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_LEAST64_MODIFIER}\par
131 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_LEAST32_MODIFIER}\par
132 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_LEAST16_MODIFIER}\par
133 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INTPTR_MODIFIER}\par
134 {\cf20  *}\par
135 {\cf20  *       are strings which have been defined as the modifiers required}\par
136 {\cf20  *       for the "d", "u" and "x" printf formats to correctly output}\par
137 {\cf20  *       (u)intmax_t, (u)int64_t, (u)int32_t, (u)int16_t, (u)least64_t,}\par
138 {\cf20  *       (u)least32_t, (u)least16_t and (u)intptr_t types respectively.}\par
139 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INTPTR_MODIFIER is not defined for some systems which}\par
140 {\cf20  *       provide their own stdint.h.  PRINTF_INT64_MODIFIER is not}\par
141 {\cf20  *       defined if INT64_MAX is not defined.  These are an extension}\par
142 {\cf20  *       beyond what C99 specifies must be in stdint.h.}\par
143 {\cf20  *}\par
144 {\cf20  *       In addition, the following macros are defined:}\par
145 {\cf20  *}\par
146 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INTMAX_HEX_WIDTH}\par
147 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT64_HEX_WIDTH}\par
148 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT32_HEX_WIDTH}\par
149 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT16_HEX_WIDTH}\par
150 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT8_HEX_WIDTH}\par
151 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INTMAX_DEC_WIDTH}\par
152 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT64_DEC_WIDTH}\par
153 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT32_DEC_WIDTH}\par
154 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_INT16_DEC_WIDTH}\par
155 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_UINT8_DEC_WIDTH}\par
156 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_UINTMAX_DEC_WIDTH}\par
157 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_UINT64_DEC_WIDTH}\par
158 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_UINT32_DEC_WIDTH}\par
159 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_UINT16_DEC_WIDTH}\par
160 {\cf20  *       PRINTF_UINT8_DEC_WIDTH}\par
161 {\cf20  *}\par
162 {\cf20  *       Which specifies the maximum number of characters required to}\par
163 {\cf20  *       print the number of that type in either hexadecimal or decimal.}\par
164 {\cf20  *       These are an extension beyond what C99 specifies must be in}\par
165 {\cf20  *       stdint.h.}\par
166 {\cf20  *}\par
167 {\cf20  *  Compilers tested (all with 0 warnings at their highest respective}\par
168 {\cf20  *  settings): Borland Turbo C 2.0, WATCOM C/C++ 11.0 (16 bits and 32}\par
169 {\cf20  *  bits), Microsoft Visual C++ 6.0 (32 bit), Microsoft Visual Studio}\par
170 {\cf20  *  .net (VC7), Intel C++ 4.0, GNU gcc v3.3.3}\par
171 {\cf20  *}\par
